    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • XPENG Reveals G9L at the 2026 Paris Motor Show, Dubbed As the Ultimate \"Large SUV for the AI Era\"\n2027 Ford Bronco Hit With Less Welcome Equipment Changes, 2.7L EcoBoost V6 Dropped Entirely\n102 photos\nPhoto: Ford / edited\nThe 2027 Ford Bronco is not available with the 2.7-liter EcoBoost V6 that used to bridge the gap between the 2.3 and the 3.0 mills for model year 2026. In non-Raptor grades, the new midranger adds 50 horsepower and 25 pound-feet (34 Newton-meters) of twist over the 2.7 it replaces.\nBeyond the discontinuation of the 2.7-liter EcoBoost V6, the Ford Motor Company has also removed the Stroppe Edition. The dealer order guide records a few interesting equipment changes. First and foremost, said order guide lists the 380-hp V6 as optional for the Outer Banks, Heritage, and four-door Badlands. It becomes standard on the new Filson specification.\nSlotting below the punchier Raptor engine, the 3.0 introduced for 2027 makes a healthy 440 pound-feet (597 Newton-meters) at full chatter. That puts the lesser tune on the same level as the Raptor in terms of peak torque, but rubber, suspension, and setup continue to serve as differentiators.\nCompared to the old 2.7 engine, the 3.0 should improve the driving experience in pretty much every imaginable scenario. Whether it's similarly efficient or a bit thirstier, the EPA will clarify that in due time. The 2.3-liter EcoBoost I4 remains the standard mill on the Base and Big Bend, manual and all. In two-pedal realm, automatic Broncos all feature 10-speed transmissions.\nThe new Filson grade receives the 3.0-liter engine as standard, leaning into a more tailored cabin full of leather and vinyl upholstery, complete with heated and ventilated front seats, power adjustment, and some distinctive trim here and there. The four-door Filson sweetens the deal with 35-inch tires and power running boards. Iron Sands and Field Green distinguish it from the rest of the range.\nPhoto: Ford To reach dealers in early 2027, the Filson will be joined by the RTR. Taking a completely different route, this fellow pairs the 2.3 with an anti-lag setup, upgraded cooling, and the 10-speed auto to the detriment of the 7-speed manual. Its wide-track HOSS 1.0 suspension combines a lift with 33-inch rubber on beadlock-capable wheels.\nThe optional Sasquatch package with RTR suspension brings forth 35s, HOSS 3.0 suspension with Fox goodies, and the heavy-duty modular front bumper, creating a desert-focused alternative to the high-speed Raptor and the rock-crawling Badlands. Although pricing has yet to be formalized, it's understood that customers will have to pony up more than $50,000 to get the RTR.\nA heated steering wheel joins the Big Bend Mid Package, and a 5G modem becomes standard across the lineup in lieu of the previous 4G system. Oh, and by the way, Neptune Blue paint replaces Velocity Blue. The Outer Banks Lux Package, meanwhile, switches from leather to premium vinyl, whereas the Outer Banks Mid Package moves to cloth and loses the power front seats.\nBadlands adds Marine Grade Vinyl, and there's also a power-adjustable option on four-door models. The pompously named vinyl upholstery is promoted as being durable and easy to clean. But truth be told, replacing leather with vinyl is a trade-off rather than an upgrade.\nAttached below, the order guide further shows the Big Bend Mid Package losing the dual smart-charging USB ports and remote start system. Similar USB and remote start deletions affect other packages, and LED signature headlamps plus LED taillamps moved off the standard equipment list for the Outer Banks and Badlands. Even the B&O audio setup included with some Lux packages drops from 12 to 10 speakers.\nA 12-speaker B&O system is available separately. Given the aforementioned, the 2027 model year Bronco is best viewed as a case of win some, lose some.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nFord Bronco 2027 Ford Bronco Ford V6 SUV\nDownload: 2027 Ford Bronco order guide (US market) (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1968 Dodge Coronet Parked for Decades Gets Second Chance, It's a Rare R/T Convertible",
